<p class='text'>I have spent the past week refactoring my deployer.</p>
<p class='text'>It's always difficult to quantify the amount of effort that goes into any particular task, and units of time really don't convey much, especially when you are probably translating that to 40 hours, which it definitely hasn't been (yet).</p>
<p class='text'>But this is a big refactoring.  If you think of refactoring in terms of extracting variables or changing method signatures, this is not what I'm talking about.  But in terms of the original meaning, it definitely comes under the heading of "refactoring".</p>
<p class='text'>The term comes from the idea in mathematics that, for example, the number 72 can be described as 8<b>9 or as 6</b>12.  Although these seem very different, they are both <i>correct</i> factorizations of 72.  It's just a question of how you assemble the prime factors of 72 (2<b>2</b>2<b>3</b>3) into bigger units (2<b>2</b>2 and 3<b>3, or 2</b>3 and 2<b>2</b>3).</p>
<p class='text'>It's the same with software.  Something can be reasonably described as a <i>refactoring</i> if the structure of the program changes (by any amount) but its observed external behaviours do <i>not</i> change.  In other words, the code and the unit tests may change out of all recognition, but any tests of the whole system should pass without change.  If any of those need changing, this is no longer a refactoring, but either an enhancement or a breaking change.</p>
<p class='text'>In line with accepted best practices, I do a lot of refactoring as I go along.  So, how then is it possible that I find myself in this situation where I have spent a whole week doing major refactorings?  From a "project" perspective, I have now been stalled for a week.  I had reached a significant milestone - where my deployer was able to deploy a website from an S3 bucket using cloudfront - and nothing external has changed since then.</p>
<p class='text'>The reason is very simple and it's commonly called <i>incremental design</i>.  That is, I started off a month or so ago with a vague idea of what I wanted to do and how I wanted my deployer to operate (you can read about that elsewhere here).  And I knew that there would be challenges to do with representation of things.  But any one example - for example, the step to read an environment variable, or ensuring a bucket exists - doesn't really give you enough information to make <i>all</i> the relevant design decisions.  When you reach a point where you have enough information - and enough system tests to check that through - you can stop and assess where the sharp edges and corners are.  And then you can spend a week smoothing them off.</p>
<p class='text'>For me, at least, this wasn't possible a couple of weeks ago.  In fact, over the course of the past week my ideas have become more and more refined.  And each time I have interrupted one refactoring to go and do another, leaving a paper trail of "unfinished thoughts" behind me.  The thing is, of course, that it is always easier to make changes (most obviously, adding new features, but also doing refactorings) once the underlying code has been sorted out.</p>
<p class='text'>I've done this sort of thing before, of course, although generally it ends up with everything being a broken mess for several days if not weeks as I blaze a housefire through code that cannot easily be transformed.  I've been relatively surprised by how easy this one has been, especially since the Go environment does not have the same support for refactoring that I am now used to in Java (of course, neither did C++ back in the 90s when I learnt my trade - all I had was Emacs, slow compile times and CVS).</p>
<p class='text'>So while there are many different ways in which we can use refactoring, I would offer these gradations:</p>
<p class='text'>* simple refactorings - what we do all the time, often not even breaking unit tests;* rewirings - a major, often multi-day, refactoring, but that (almost) every check in builds correctly and all the tests pass; this can be done on trunk;* housefires - a massive refactoring that breaks everything for days or weeks, but eventually comes good; this obviously has to be done on a branch and then usually involves a significant merge at the end;* just not a refactoring - anything that breaks existing behaviour or system tests.</p>
